\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}Unless a section says otherwise, ${\mathbb{N}}=\{1,2,\ldots\}$,
${\mathbb{Z}}$ is the ring of integers, ${\mathbb{Q}},{\mathbb{R}},{\mathbb{C}}$ are the rational, real, and complex
fields, $[N]=\{1,\ldots,\lfloor N\rfloor\}$, and $\log$ is the natural
logarithm. For real functions of a parameter tending to infinity,
$f\ll g$ and $f=O(g)$ mean $|f|\leq Cg$ eventually for some fixed $C$;
$f\gg g$ reverses this comparison; $f=o(g)$ means $f/g\to0$;
$f\sim g$ means $f/g\to1$; and $f\asymp g$ means both order bounds.
A subscript on $O$ or $\ll$ specifies allowed constant dependence.
The expression $n^{o(1)}$ in an upper bound means growth smaller than
$n^\epsilon$ for each fixed $\epsilon>0$. Local definitions govern any
exception, finite-field convention, or use of the same symbol for another
object. An asymptotic claim is not automatically an effective algorithm.



An ordinary basis permits repeated summands. Its restricted order requires pairwise distinct elements within each representation and is the least uniform bound on the number used. All basis statements concern sufficiently large positive target integers. \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]} \href{https://www.erdosproblems.com/338}{[S2]}

\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
The restricted order of a basis is the least integer $t$ (if it exists) such that every large integer is the sum of at most $t$ distinct summands from $A$. What are necessary and sufficient conditions that this exists? Can it be bounded (when it exists) in terms of the order of the basis? What are necessary and sufficient conditions that this is equal to the order of the basis? \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
When can every sufficiently large integer be represented by boundedly many distinct basis elements, and how does that bound compare with ordinary basis order?

